                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 862,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1166,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Cuts a signal into short overlapping windows and finds the frequencies in each: a picture with frequency one way and time the other, bright where there is energy. It shows how the content of a signal changes - a rhythm speeding up, a tremor that comes and goes, a vibration that starts at one moment and stops at another.\n\nA single spectrum (np.fft.rfft, scipy.welch) mixes the whole signal together; the spectrogram keeps the order of events.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.spectrogram <window: int> <sample rate>\nDefaults: window 64, sample rate 60.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nscipy.spectrogram 128 100\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe signal: a list or 1-D array. Every input is analysed and sent.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second, so that frequencies come out in cycles per second and times in seconds.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many samples in each window. A long window tells close frequencies apart but blurs when things happen; a short one is sharp in time but coarse in frequency. The frequencies are spaced sample rate / window apart.\n\noverlap (float box, also an inlet):\nHow much each window overlaps the last, as a fraction: 0.5 (the default) starts each halfway into the previous one.\n\ndecibels:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default here), the power is given in decibels (10 log10 of the power), which shows faint and strong content together; off, as plain power.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npower:\nOne row per frequency, one column per window: the picture.\n\nfrequencies:\nThe frequency of each row, from 0 up to half the sample rate.\n\ntimes:\nThe time at the centre of each window. Sent first, then the frequencies, then the power.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_chirp subpatcher makes 10 seconds of a wave that gets steadily faster, from 0 to about 20 cycles a second. In the spectrogram time runs left to right and frequency top to bottom (0 at the top): a bright line running down from the top left, from about 1 cycle a second in the first window to about 18 in the last.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.welch gives one steady spectrum for the whole signal. np.fft.rfft gives the spectrum of one window. np.sliding_window_view and np.fft.rfft can build the same picture step by step. spectrum analyses a live stream.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1150,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Cuts a signal into short overlapping windows and finds the frequencies in each: a picture with frequency one way and time the other, bright where there is energy. It shows how the content of a signal changes - a rhythm speeding up, a tremor that comes and goes, a vibration that starts at one moment and stops at another.\n\nA single spectrum (np.fft.rfft, scipy.welch) mixes the whole signal together; the spectrogram keeps the order of events.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.spectrogram <window: int> <sample rate>\nDefaults: window 64, sample rate 60.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nscipy.spectrogram 128 100\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe signal: a list or 1-D array. Every input is analysed and sent.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second, so that frequencies come out in cycles per second and times in seconds.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many samples in each window. A long window tells close frequencies apart but blurs when things happen; a short one is sharp in time but coarse in frequency. The frequencies are spaced sample rate / window apart.\n\noverlap (float box, also an inlet):\nHow much each window overlaps the last, as a fraction: 0.5 (the default) starts each halfway into the previous one.\n\ndecibels:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default here), the power is given in decibels (10 log10 of the power), which shows faint and strong content together; off, as plain power.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npower:\nOne row per frequency, one column per window: the picture.\n\nfrequencies:\nThe frequency of each row, from 0 up to half the sample rate.\n\ntimes:\nThe time at the centre of each window. Sent first, then the frequencies, then the power.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_chirp subpatcher makes 10 seconds of a wave that gets steadily faster, from 0 to about 20 cycles a second. In the spectrogram time runs left to right and frequency top to bottom (0 at the top): a bright line running down from the top left, from about 1 cycle a second in the first window to about 18 in the last.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.welch gives one steady spectrum for the whole signal. np.fft.rfft gives the spectrum of one window. np.sliding_window_view and np.fft.rfft can build the same picture step by step. spectrum analyses a live stream."
                },
